1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,649
What if your AI kept working while you slept?

2
00:00:02,699 --> 00:00:06,331
Perplexity says its new Teammates do exactly that.

3
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:09,276
Perplexity launched Teammates in early November.

4
00:00:09,326 --> 00:00:11,700
You give one a role and a goal.

5
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:15,684
It plans the tasks, and brings finished work back for your review.

6
00:00:15,734 --> 00:00:17,319
The catch is the price.

7
00:00:17,369 --> 00:00:21,729
Teammates needs a Perplexity Max subscription, two hundred dollars a month.

8
00:00:21,779 --> 00:00:23,544
Is it free, Muja?

9
00:00:23,594 --> 00:00:26,504
Reports say only during a launch promotion.

10
00:00:26,554 --> 00:00:27,977
Credits come later.

11
00:00:28,027 --> 00:00:30,591
An alarm clock, Muja?

12
00:00:30,641 --> 00:00:36,848
Fun fact: in seventeen eighty-seven, Levi Hutchins built America's first alarm clock.

13
00:00:36,898 --> 00:00:40,175
It only rang at four in the morning, because that was when he woke up.

14
00:00:40,225 --> 00:00:41,151
Okay...

15
00:00:41,201 --> 00:00:42,619
back to our story.

16
00:00:42,669 --> 00:00:43,697
What looks good?

17
00:00:43,747 --> 00:00:48,492
Teammates remember their job, and can work inside tools like Slack and GitHub.

18
00:00:48,542 --> 00:00:49,829
What worries me?

19
00:00:49,879 --> 00:00:53,637
There are no independent tests yet, and it is still a research preview.

20
00:00:53,687 --> 00:00:55,302
So what should Priya do?

21
00:00:55,352 --> 00:00:57,765
Pick one small, boring job.

22
00:00:57,815 --> 00:00:59,720
Keep the access read-only.

23
00:00:59,770 --> 00:01:03,000
And have a human approve anything that leaves the building.

24
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:06,212
My take: treat it as a pilot, not a purchase.

25
00:01:06,262 --> 00:01:08,564
Test it for a month with your own numbers.

26
00:01:08,614 --> 00:01:11,005
The full review is on my blog.
